<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capo Colonna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lacinian promontory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Capo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> is a cape on the Ionian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crotone">Crotone</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was the site of the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> Lacinia, the main religious centre of the Greek cities of southern Italy, whose temple of the 5th century BCE was later plundered for building stone. A single standing Doric column survives and gives the cape its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capo-colonna.jpg" alt="Capo Colonna" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capo_Colonna2_retouched.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
